    ...sec(r'the idea'),
    ...text(r'Adding a dependency is a context switch with five steps: leave the editor, search crates.io, copy a name, find out which features exist, run cargo add with the right flags. The crate-adding flow here is inspired by the dependency handling of the JetBrains IDEs, which the author still uses, and the README says so plainly while stating that the plugin is independent and not affiliated. The ambition is to do those steps where the cursor is, with pickers for the crate and its features, and to make the editor notice the situations that cost the most time: a crate that is not a dependency, a feature that is switched off, a name that rust-analyzer cannot import because the crate has not been added.'),
    blank,
    ...text(r'There is a deeper reason it works in Neovim at all. rust-analyzer only knows what is in the dependency graph; it cannot import from a crate that is not there, and its diagnostics for unresolved names are off in the fast tier because they gave 82 false errors on a real workspace (the plan document in xynovim says so). So the plugin brings its own, narrower knowledge. It reads cargo metadata, the unpacked sources in ~/.cargo/registry, and the crates.io sparse index; and it serves its answers through the same code-action menu that rust-analyzer uses.'),
    blank,
    ...sec(r'a tour in three moments'),
    ...bullet(r'1. the hint', r'You type rand::random::<u8>(). After 300 ms of quiet a tree-sitter scan of the buffer finds a path head that is not std, not a dependency of this package, not a local module, not bound by a use and not a type. The word rand gets a HINT diagnostic: crate ’rand’ is not a dependency. The scan is deliberately conservative: a miss is cheap, a false alarm is not, so a file with a glob import from anywhere that could hide the name says nothing at all.'),
    ...bullet(r'2. the action', r'The code-action menu now has Add crate rand, preferred, and Add crate rand (choose features and target). The first runs cargo add rand -p <your package> (--dev for files under tests/, benches/ and examples/) and reports the line cargo wrote. The second opens the :CrateAdd picker flow for that name. A language server that is a Lua table, not a process, serves both.'),
    ...bullet(r'3. the import', r'If the unresolved name is an item, such as Uuid, and rust-analyzer offers nothing, a source registered with xyno-imports.nvim proposes crates whose name equals or contains it, most downloaded first, labelled as guesses because crates.io is searched by crate name, not by what a crate exports. Accept one and the plugin adds it, polls rust-analyzer for up to a minute until it offers the import, and applies the exact path.'),
    ...sec(r'what it adds to the editor'),
    ...bullet(r':CrateAdd [query]', r'search crates.io, pick the crate with version, download count and description, pick its features (default preselected, unticking it means --no-default-features), pick the target (workspace member and table; entries of [workspace.dependencies] are marked) and run cargo add.'),
    ...bullet(r':CrateFeatures [crate]', r'an explorer for one dependency: declared and effective features, what each unlocks (the gated public items found in the crate’s own source), the default set, an "unused?" hint for a feature none of whose items appears in your workspace, and toggling by editing Cargo.toml.'),
    ...bullet(r'the missing-feature action', r'rustc says cannot find derive macro Serialize; the plugin looks the name up in the dependency’s source, follows the #[cfg(feature = "...")] gate through the [features] table, and offers the smallest list of features to enable.'),
    ...bullet(r'import from nowhere', r'add the crate and import in one step, with a bounded wait and honest labels.'),
